{"success": true, "data": {"stdout": "{"id": 0, "depth": 0, "text": "Anarchism is a political philosophy and movement that is skeptical of all justifications for authority and seeks to abolish the institutions it claims maintain unnecessary coercion and hierarchy, typically including nation-states, and capitalism. Anarchism advocates for the replacement of the state with stateless societies and voluntary free associations. As a historically left-wing movement, this reading of anarchism is placed on the farthest left of the political spectrum, usually described as the libertarian wing of the socialist movement (libertarian socialism).\n\nHumans have lived in societies without formal hierarchies long before the establishment of states, realms, or empires. With the rise of organised hierarchical bodies, scepticism toward authority also rose. Although traces of anarchist ideas are found all throughout history, modern anarchism emerged from the Enlightenment. During the latter half of the 19th and the first decades of the 20th century, the anarchist movement flourished in most parts of the world and had a significant role in workers' struggles for emancipation. Various anarchist schools of thought formed during this period. Anarchists have taken part in several revolutions, most notably in the Paris Commune, the Russian Civil War and the Spanish Civil War, whose end marked the end of the classical era of anarchism. In the last decades of", "seed_id": 0, "token_count": 256, "model": "llama-3.2-3b-instruct"}
{"id": 1, "depth": 1, "text": "the 20th century, anarchist movements began to be repressed, and many anarchists were imprisoned or forced into exile. Despite this, the anarchist movement continued to evolve, and new forms of activism and organizing emerged. The 1960s and 1970s saw a resurgence of interest in anarchism, particularly among the counterculture movement, with the rise of anarchist collectives, free schools, and communes. This period also saw the emergence of new anarchist schools of thought, such as anarcho-primitivism and eco-anarchism.\n\nIn recent years, anarchism has experienced a revival, particularly among young people and in the context of the global justice movement. The rise of social media and online platforms has facilitated the spread of anarchist ideas and networks, and has enabled anarchists to connect with each other and organize across borders. However, anarchism remains a marginalized and stigmatized ideology, and many anarchists continue to face repression and violence from state and corporate forces.\n\nDespite these challenges, anarchism remains a powerful and enduring philosophy, one that continues to inspire and empower people around the world to challenge authority and build alternative forms of social organization. Anarchist theory and practice have been influenced by a wide range of traditions, from ancient Greek philosophy to modern-day feminist", "seed_id": 0, "token_count": 256, "model": "llama-3.2-3b-instruct"}
{"id": 2, "depth": 2, "text": "activism, and have incorporated elements from various cultural and intellectual movements. Anarchist thinkers such as Pierre-Joseph Proudhon, Mikhail Bakunin, and Emma Goldman have been instrumental in shaping the ideology, while contemporary anarchists continue to draw on a diverse range of sources, including the Situationist International, the Situationist movement, and the work of radical thinkers such as Murray Bookchin and David Graeber.\n\nThe rise of anarchist feminism and queer theory has also led to a reevaluation of anarchist principles and practice, highlighting issues of intersectionality and social justice. Anarchist ecologists and environmentalists have emphasized the need for a more sustainable and eco-friendly approach to social organization, while anarchist technologists and hackers have explored the potential of technology to empower and liberate individuals and communities.\n\nDespite these developments, anarchism remains a decentralized and diverse movement, with a wide range of perspectives and approaches. Anarchist collectives and networks continue to thrive, providing alternative spaces for social organization and community building. The internet and social media have enabled anarchists to connect and coordinate across borders, facilitating the spread of anarchist ideas and the growth of a global anarchist network.", "seed_id": 0, "token_count": 234, "model": "llama-3.2-3b-instruct"}
{"id": 3, "depth": 3, "text": "As a result, anarchist communities and initiatives have proliferated, addressing issues such as housing, healthcare, and education, and providing alternative models for social organization and economic activity. Anarchist artists and musicians have also been instrumental in shaping the movement's cultural landscape, using their work to challenge dominant narratives and promote anarchist values.\n\nThe growth of anarchist education and community programs has helped to build capacity and foster a sense of solidarity among anarchists, while the increasing visibility of anarchist activism has led to a rise in mainstream media coverage and public engagement. However, this increased visibility has also brought new challenges, including the need to navigate the complexities of mainstream politics and media.\n\nDespite these challenges, anarchist activists continue to be driven by a commitment to creating a more just and equitable world, and their efforts have inspired a wide range of social movements and initiatives, from anti-globalization protests to environmental activism. As anarchism continues to evolve and adapt to new contexts and challenges, its core principles of mutual aid, non-hierarchy, and social justice remain a powerful force for change.", "seed_id": 0, "token_count": 210, "model": "llama-3.2-3b-instruct"}
{"id": 4, "depth": 4, "text": "This evolution has led to the emergence of new forms of anarchist practice, such as autonomous zones, community land trusts, and cooperatives, which are being used to build alternative economies and social systems. The growth of online platforms and social media has also enabled anarchists to connect and organize more effectively, creating new networks and coalitions that transcend geographical boundaries.\n\nAt the same time, anarchist theory and practice continue to be shaped by a diverse range of influences, from indigenous and decolonial perspectives to feminist and queer theory. This diversity has enriched anarchist thought and practice, highlighting the importance of intersectionality and inclusivity in building more just and equitable societies.\n\nAs anarchist activism continues to grow and evolve, it is also being shaped by new forms of resistance and protest, such as direct action and non-violent direct action. These tactics are being used to challenge systems of oppression and inequality, and to promote social change through a variety of channels, from direct action to electoral politics.\n\nUltimately, the anarchist movement is driven by a vision of a world without states, markets, and other forms of domination, in which people are free to organize and live in accordance with their own values and needs. This vision is still a work in progress, and it will require continued effort and creativity to realize.", "seed_id": 0, "token_count": 254, "model": "llama-3.2-3b-instruct"}
